> An Indian coaching institute ranks students by marks descending
    > then by name ascending for ties. This uses a custom Comparator.
=======================================================================================================
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.TreeSet;

class Student {
    String name;
    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " (" + marks + ")";
    }
}

public class JeeRankList {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Custom comparator: sort by marks DESC, then name ASC for ties
        Comparator<Student> rankComparator = (a, b) -> {
            if (a.marks != b.marks) {
                return Integer.compare(b.marks, a.marks);   // higher marks first
            }
            return a.name.compareTo(b.name);                // alphabetical tie-break
        };

        TreeSet<Student> rankList = new TreeSet<>(rankComparator);

        rankList.add(new Student("Aarav",  292));
        rankList.add(new Student("Diya",   305));
        rankList.add(new Student("Rohan",  305));
        rankList.add(new Student("Kavya",  288));
        rankList.add(new Student("Ishaan", 292));

        System.out.println("JEE Rank List (top first):");
        int rank = 1;
        for (Student s : rankList) {
            System.out.println("  Rank " + rank++ + ": " + s);
        }

        System.out.println("\nTop ranker: " + rankList.first());
        System.out.println("Lowest in list: " + rankList.last());
    }
}
=======================================================================================================
o/p
JEE Rank List (top first):
  Rank 1: Diya (305)
  Rank 2: Rohan (305)
  Rank 3: Aarav (292)
  Rank 4: Ishaan (292)
  Rank 5: Kavya (288)

Top ranker: Diya (305)
Lowest in list: Kavya (288)
=======================================================================================================
Why TreeSet? The comparator is honored on every insertion — 
every element is inserted into the right position automatically. 
With a List, you'd have to call Collections.sort() every time marks change.

⚠️ Subtle gotcha: TreeSet uses the comparator to decide uniqueness. 
If two students have the same marks and same name, the second would be rejected as a duplicate.
